
<p>Although this differential equation is difficult to solve in general, when the angle \(\theta\) is small you can use the small angle approximation \(\sin\theta\approx\theta\) and instead solve
\[
\frac{d^2\theta}{dt^2}+\frac{g}\ell\theta=0.
\]
 Solving the differential equation, we see that
\[
\theta=C_1\cos\bigg(\sqrt{\frac g\ell}t\bigg)+C_2\sin\bigg(\sqrt{\frac g\ell}t\bigg).
\]
Since sine and cosine have period \(2\pi\), the pendulum itself will have period (approximately)
\[
2\pi\sqrt{\frac\ell g}.
\]</p>



<h2>A simple example</h2>

<p>
Since we've worked quite abstractly so far, let's solve an explicit problem using Lagrangian mechanics.
</p>

<p>
Suppose that we're standing in a room, with a flat floor. Choose a coordinate system so that the floor is described by the plane \(x_3 = 0.\)
</p>

<p>
You brought a ball of mass \(2\text{kg}\) with you, and toss it; say your toss gives the ball an initial velocity \(\vec v(0) = (1\text{m/s}, 1\text{m/s}, 0),\) and you threw the ball from initial position \(\vec x(0) = (0, 0, 1\text{m}).\) We want to compute the position \(\vec x(t)\) of the velocity \(t\) seconds after you threw it. 
</p>

<p>
Let's say the only force acting on the ball is gravity. In his study of gravity on Earth, Newton computed that the potential energy of gravity is 
\[V(t) = 10\frac{\text{m}}{\text{s}^2} \cdot 2\text{kg} \cdot x_3(t),\]
where \(x_3(t)\) appears because it is the height of the ball above the ground. 
</p>

<p>
Thus our Lagrangian is 
\[L = (v_1(t)^2 + v_2(t)^2 + v_3(t)^2) \cdot 1\text{kg} - 20x_3(t)\frac{\text{m}\cdot\text{kg}}{\text{s}^2}\]
</p>

<p>

</p>

<p>
  The Euler-Lagrange equation for the first coordinate tells us
  \[
  \frac{\partial L}{\partial x_1}=0
  \]
  equals
  \[
  \frac d{dt}\bigg(\frac{\partial L}{\partial v_1}\bigg)=\frac d{dt}(mv_1)=m\frac{dv_1}{dt}.
  \]
  So the Euler-Lagrange equations know that the ball shouldn't accelerate in the \(x_1\)-direction! This matches our physical intuition, since gravity is only acting in the vertical direction; the velocity of the object in horizontal directions shouldn't change.
  The Euler-Lagrange equation for the second coordinate also tells us
  \[
  m\frac{dv_2}{dt}=0,
  \]
  which makes sense from the same physical intuition.
</p>

<p>
  Finally, the Euler-Lagrange equation for the third coordinate tells us
  \[
  \frac{\partial L}{\partial x_3}=-mg
  \]
  equals
  \[
  \frac d{dt}\bigg(\frac{\partial L}{\partial v_3}\bigg)=\frac d{dt}(mv_3)=m\frac{dv_3}{dt}.
  \]
  In other words, 
  \[
  m\frac{dv_3}{dt}=-mg,
  \]
  which tells us
  \[
  \frac{dv_3}{dt}=-g.
  \]
  This is just saying the ball accelerates downwards by \(g\), which is also exactly what we expect!
</p>


